# Push and build

When the user says **push and build** (or clearly the same intent):

## Order (critical)

Do steps in this order so **nothing is left local** after push:

1. **Version bump** (section 0)
2. **Android sync** (section 1) — Capacitor build + `cap sync` may modify `android/` files
3. **Git commit + push** (section 2) — stage **everything** including sync-generated gradle files

Never push then sync. Sync first, then commit so `capacitor.build.gradle` / `capacitor.settings.gradle` and any other sync output are included in the same commit.

## 0. Version bump (required every time)

Before sync/commit, bump the app version. This is **critical** — players must be able to report which build they are on.

### Source of truth

- `constants/appVersion.ts` — `APP_VERSION` (`major.minor.patch`, currently starting from `0.1.0`) and `APP_VERSION_CODE` (integer)

Also keep these in sync with `APP_VERSION` / `APP_VERSION_CODE`:

- `components/SettingsPopup.tsx` — must display `v${APP_VERSION}` (import from `appVersion.ts`; do not hardcode)
- `android/app/build.gradle` — `versionName` = `APP_VERSION`, `versionCode` = `APP_VERSION_CODE`
- `package.json` — `"version"` field = `APP_VERSION`
- `index.html` — `<meta name="version" content="...">` = `APP_VERSION`

### What to bump

Read the current `APP_VERSION` and the commits / diff being shipped, then:

1. **Patch** (`0.1.x` → `0.1.x+1`) — default for small changes: bug fixes, tweaks, QoL, polish, copy, balancing, SFX, layout.
2. **Minor** (`0.x.0` → `0.(x+1).0`) — larger updates: new features, significant systems, major UX flows, anything players would call “a new update.” Reset patch to `0`.
3. **Major** (`x.0.0`) — only if the user explicitly asks for a major bump (breaking / relaunch-scale).

Always increment **`APP_VERSION_CODE` by +1** on every push-and-build (Play Store requires a new integer each upload), regardless of patch vs minor.

If unclear whether the change is patch or minor, ask in one short question; if they already said “small” / “bug fix” / “QoL”, use patch; if they said “feature” / “new system” / described a large change, use minor.

Do **not** skip the version bump even if the user forgot to mention it.

## 1. Android sync (before git)

From the project root, run the Capacitor web build and sync so Android Studio has the latest assets/code **and** so generated android gradle/plugin files exist before commit:

- `npm run build:capacitor`
- `npx cap sync android`

(Or equivalently `npm run cap:sync` if that script matches.)

After sync, re-check `git status` — include any modified `android/` files (especially `android/app/capacitor.build.gradle` and `android/capacitor.settings.gradle`) in the upcoming commit.

## 2. Git

Follow the normal commit + push flow (status/diff/log, stage relevant files **including sync artifacts**, commit with their message if given, push to origin). Do not commit secrets. Only commit when they asked to push/commit. Include the version bump files and Capacitor sync output in the **same** commit when possible.

After push, confirm `git status` is clean (or only unrelated untracked junk). Briefly remind them of the new version string (e.g. `v0.1.1`).
